  <iati-identifier>XM-DAC-41302-PMR26-JPN101</iati-identifier>
  <reporting-org ref="XM-DAC-41302" type="40">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">International Labour Organization (ILO)</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Japanese enterprises are better equipped with knowledge and skills to advance responsible business practices in line with the principles of the ILO MNE Declaration</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">As one of the major home countries of multinational enterprises in the region, Japanese enterprises play an increasingly important role in promoting sustainable development through their operations, supply chains, and business relationships both domestically and globally. To address challenges related to responsible business conduct and decent work in domestic and global supply chains, this outcome aims to strengthen the knowledge and capacity of the constituents and key stakeholders, including Japanese enterprises, workers, workers&#x2019; organizations, employer and business membership organizations (EBMOs), and government institutions, to implement responsible and sustainable business practices in line with the principles of the ILO MNE Declaration, including by promoting gender equality and respect for fundamental principles and rights at work, during the 2026-27 biennium.
The expected result is that Japanese enterprises are better equipped to integrate international labour standards and responsible business conduct into their operations, supply chains, and business relationships, including through enhanced awareness, due diligence approaches, and meaningful consultation.
The primary beneficiaries are Japanese enterprises, workers, workers&#x2019; organizations, EBMOs, as well as government institutions involved in promoting responsible business conduct and decent work.
The strategy is implemented through collaboration with workers&#x2019; and employers&#x2019; organizations, government institutions, international organizations, and other stakeholders engaged in advancing responsible business conduct, sustainable enterprises, and decent work.</narrative>
  </description>
